## Recommended Naming Convention for Subdomain

We recommend that the subdomain should only contain letters (a-z), digits (0-9), and hyphens (not at the start or end). The provider does not prevent using other naming patterns, but it will raise a warning if the subdomain does not follow this convention.

## Pending Deletion

When a subaccount is deleted, the Terraform provider will try to execute a delete of the subaccount. If this delete fails, we will retry with force-delete enabled if the error message indicates that active resources are present. This will delete all data contained within the subaccount. The deletion is not executed immediately when the force delete is triggered. Instead, a pending deletion period of several days starts. After this period, the subaccount is permanently and irreversibly deleted. Be aware that during this period costs for usage and allocated resources may still apply and are billed. During this pending deletion period, you can restore the subaccount via the SAP BTP cockpit, via the btp CLI or via the Terraform action `btp_restore_subaccount`.

Force deletion is available only if the force deletion of subaccounts is enabled in the global account settings and if the subaccount isn't marked as used for production. For information about how to change global account settings, see https://help.sap.com/docs/btp/sap-business-technology-platform/managing-global-accounts-using-cockpit

Be aware, that the subaccount is removed from the Terraform state file when the subaccount is in the state of a pending deletion. This means that the subaccount will not be tracked by Terraform anymore. If you want to track the subaccount again, you can import the subaccount again after it has been restored.

## Skip Auto Entitlement

The `skip_auto_entitlement` attribute affects subaccount creation only. Setting it to `true` skips automatic assignment of base entitlements. Omitting it or setting it to `false` includes the standard auto-assigned plans.

For an existing subaccount, Terraform handles changes as follows:

| Change | Behavior |
|---|---|
| Omitted → `false`, or `false` → omitted | Updates Terraform state only; existing entitlements are unchanged |
| Omitted or `false` → `true` | Requires replacement |
| `true` → `false` or omitted | Requires replacement |

“Omitted” also includes an explicit `null` value. If the new value is unknown during planning, the provider conservatively marks the subaccount for replacement.

**Replacement deletes and recreates the subaccount and can affect its resources and data.** Terraform identifies the replacement during planning. Review `terraform plan` before applying changes, including when removing this attribute from your configuration. See [Pending Deletion](#pending-deletion) for deletion behavior.

## Restriction

### Move of Subaccounts

The resource does not support the move of the subaccount to a new parent account (directory or global account). An update of the `parent_id` attribute will cause a deletion and recreation of the resource including the resources that depend on it.

For further details please refer to [Limitation - Move of Subaccounts](https://github.com/SAP/terraform-provider-btp/blob/main/guides/MOVESUBACCOUNT.md).

### Import of Subaccounts

Import sets `skip_auto_entitlement` to `null` because the API does not expose its original value. After import, leaving the attribute omitted or setting it to `false` does not require replacement because of this attribute. Setting it to `false` records that value in Terraform state without changing existing entitlements. Setting it to `true` requires replacement.
